    id: "18",
    title: "Root Canal vs Extraction: Which Is Better for Your Tooth?",
    excerpt: "Painless root canal or pull the tooth out? Our Palghar endodontists break down cost, longevity, recovery and long-term consequences so you can choose wisely.",
    content: "When a tooth becomes badly decayed, cracked or infected, you'll typically be given two choices: a root canal treatment (RCT) to save the tooth, or an extraction to pull it out. As experienced endodontists at Dental Solutions Palghar we strongly recommend saving the natural tooth whenever possible — and here's exactly why. Cost in the short term favours extraction (₹500-₹2,000 for a simple extraction vs ₹3,500-₹10,000 for RCT + crown). But that's only half the story. A missing tooth must be replaced, otherwise neighbouring teeth tilt into the gap, the opposite tooth over-erupts, your bite collapses, and the jawbone in that area starts shrinking within 6 months. Replacing an extracted tooth with a dental implant + crown costs ₹40,000-₹60,000, and a 3-unit bridge costs ₹30,000-₹50,000. So the 'cheap' extraction actually ends up 5-10× more expensive than a root canal once you account for the replacement. Longevity strongly favours RCT: a root-canal-treated tooth with a proper crown lasts 25+ years on average, often a lifetime. Dental implants have a 95-98% 10-year success rate but require bone, surgery, and 3-6 months of healing. Bridges typically need replacement every 10-15 years and require grinding down two healthy adjacent teeth. Recovery is also much easier with RCT — most patients return to normal activities the same day with only mild soreness for 1-2 days. Extraction involves a 1-2 week healing period, dry-socket risk, and you're left without that tooth until a replacement is made (4-6 weeks for a bridge, 3-6 months for an implant). The pain factor surprises most patients: modern RCT is completely painless thanks to good local anaesthesia and rotary endodontics. The old reputation of 'root canals hurt' comes from the *infection* you had before the RCT, not the treatment itself — in fact RCT *relieves* that pain. When is extraction actually the better choice? Only in specific situations: the tooth is split vertically down to the root, there's severe bone loss from advanced gum disease, the tooth is impacted (like wisdom teeth), or the cost of saving it (RCT + post + crown + possibly a crown lengthening) exceeds the cost of an implant. Our endodontists at Dental Solutions Palghar always discuss both options honestly and recommend extraction only when RCT genuinely won't deliver long-term value. Bottom line: if your dentist tells you a tooth 'needs to come out', get a second opinion from an endodontist before agreeing. Most of the time, your natural tooth can — and should — be saved.",
