    title: "Bad Breath (Halitosis): 9 Causes Your Dentist in Palghar Can Actually Fix",
    excerpt: "Bad breath is almost always a dental problem, not a stomach problem. Here are the 9 real causes and the treatments that work.",
    content: "Persistent bad breath (halitosis) affects roughly 1 in 4 adults and almost everyone is misdiagnosed — patients spend months on probiotics, mouthwashes and stomach treatments when the real cause is usually inside the mouth. Here's the dental-truth list from Dental Solutions Palghar. 1. Tongue coating. The back of your tongue harbours 60% of oral bacteria. A tongue scraper used twice daily eliminates bad breath in 40% of patients on its own. 2. Gum disease (gingivitis and periodontitis). Pockets of infection between gum and tooth produce volatile sulphur compounds — the signature 'rotten egg' smell. Professional scaling and root planing in Palghar (₹2,500-₹6,000 depending on severity) solves this. 3. Cavities. Decayed teeth trap food and bacteria. Fillings stop the smell at the source. 4. Old or leaking fillings and crowns. Microscopic gaps trap bacteria. Replacement clears the problem. 5. Failed root canals or hidden tooth abscesses. These produce a particularly foul, persistent smell. CBCT imaging at our clinic finds these reliably. 6. Wisdom tooth pericoronitis. Partial wisdom tooth eruption traps food under the gum flap. Extraction is usually the answer. 7. Dry mouth (xerostomia). Saliva washes bacteria away — when it's reduced (from antihistamines, antidepressants, mouth breathing, diabetes, dehydration), bacteria multiply. Treatment includes hydration, sugar-free gum, prescription saliva substitutes, and sometimes adjusting medication with your physician. 8. Dirty dentures and ill-fitting bridges. Old acrylic dentures absorb odours and harbour Candida. Professional ultrasonic cleaning and reline help; sometimes replacement is needed. 9. Tonsil stones (tonsilloliths) and chronic sinusitis. These are the 10-15% of halitosis cases that aren't dental — your dentist will rule out the dental causes first and refer you appropriately. Stomach-related bad breath is rare (under 5% of cases) and is almost always accompanied by reflux symptoms, not just smell. The fastest test: book a halitosis consultation. A 20-minute exam with a halimeter (sulphur-compound meter) and tongue/periodontal assessment identifies the cause in nearly every case. Most patients are smell-free within 2-4 weeks.",
