    id: "30",
    title: "Dental Care During Pregnancy: A Trimester-by-Trimester Guide from Your Palghar Dentist",
    excerpt: "Pregnant women in Palghar can safely have most dental treatments — and skipping them carries real risks. Here's the trimester-by-trimester roadmap.",
    content: "Pregnancy hormones increase the risk of gingivitis, pregnancy tumours (benign gum overgrowths) and tooth decay — and untreated gum disease in pregnancy is linked to preterm birth and low birth weight. Yet many expectant mothers in Palghar avoid the dentist entirely out of fear that treatment will harm the baby. The opposite is true: skipping dental care during pregnancy is the real risk. Here's the safe, evidence-based plan from Dental Solutions Palghar. First trimester (weeks 1-13). Schedule a check-up and professional cleaning as soon as you confirm pregnancy. We avoid elective treatment and X-rays during this period (unless emergency) because organ development is happening. Brushing twice daily and flossing become non-negotiable — morning sickness exposes teeth to stomach acid, so rinse with water (not toothpaste) immediately after vomiting and brush 30 minutes later. Second trimester (weeks 14-27). This is the safest window for any necessary dental work — fillings, scaling and root planing, root canals, extractions and crown work can all be safely done now. Local anaesthetics (lignocaine with adrenaline) are safe and we use lead-aproned digital X-rays only when essential (radiation exposure from a single dental X-ray is less than what you receive from a day of sunlight). Third trimester (weeks 28-40). Routine and emergency care only. Long appointments become uncomfortable lying on your back, so we use a left-tilt chair position to relieve pressure on the inferior vena cava. We avoid elective cosmetic procedures and complex surgeries — these can wait until postpartum. What to tell your dentist: your due date, any medications and supplements you're taking, whether your pregnancy is high-risk, and your obstetrician's contact details for any required clearance. Safe medications during pregnancy include paracetamol for pain, amoxicillin and azithromycin for infection, and chlorhexidine mouthwash. Avoid: NSAIDs (ibuprofen, diclofenac) especially in the third trimester, tetracycline antibiotics (cause tooth discolouration in the baby), and elective whitening. After delivery, schedule a postpartum check-up — pregnancy-related gum inflammation usually resolves with professional cleaning, and any deferred treatment can now be completed. If you're pregnant and worried about a dental problem, call us — we'll triage over the phone and book you safely into the right trimester window.",
